<<<
lesser. See LESS.

lest. The idiomatic construction after l. is should, or in exalted style the pure subjunctive (l. we forget; l. he be angry). Instead of should, good writers rarely use shall, may, & might.
===
**lesser.** See ^^LESS^^.

**lest.** The idiomatic construction after *l.* is *should*, or in exalted style the pure subjunctive (*l. we forget*; *l. he be angry*). Instead of *should*, good writers rarely use *shall, may,* & *might*.
>>>

<<<
will & would after l. are merely a special form of the inability to distinguish between SHALL & will. Mistakes corresponding to those after l. are still more frequent after IN ORDER THAT.
===
*will* & *would* after *l.* are merely a special form of the inability to distinguish between ^^SHALL^^ & *will*. Mistakes corresponding to those after *l.* are still more frequent after ^^IN ORDER THAT^^.
>>>

